{
  "concept": "When two sides and a non-included angle (SSA) are given, the number of possible triangles is decided entirely by comparing the swinging side a to the altitude h = b·sin α and to side b: a < h gives none, a = h gives one right triangle, h < a < b gives two, and a ≥ b gives one.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "four side-by-side panels labeled (a), (b), (c), (d) below each",
    "panel heading 'No triangle, a < h' above (a)",
    "panel heading 'Right triangle, a = h' above (b)",
    "panel heading 'Two triangles, a > h, a < b' above (c)",
    "panel heading 'One triangle, a ≥ b' above (d)",
    "horizontal base line from vertex α (left) to vertex β (right) in each panel",
    "acute angle arc at α in every panel",
    "vertex labels α, β, γ in every panel",
    "side b drawn from α up to apex γ, labeled 'b'",
    "side a hanging from γ, labeled 'a'",
    "panel (a): short vertical a from γ stopping above the base, leaving a visible gap (no closure)",
    "panel (b): a reaching the base exactly at β with a small right-angle square at β",
    "panel (c): solid vertical altitude from γ to the base labeled 'h'",
    "panel (c): two dashed segments each labeled 'a' from γ to two different base landing points (one left of the altitude foot, one at β)",
    "panel (d): broad triangle with side a sloping from γ down to β",
    "live SSA construction area: fixed base with α arc, fixed side b to γ, altitude h guide line, and a swinging side a from γ that rotates to meet (or miss) the base, marking 0, 1, or 2 intersection points",
    "live-area readout of the current case name and the active inequality for a"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "sideA",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Side length a",
      "range": [3, 12, 0.1],
      "default": 4,
      "teaches": "With α and b held fixed, dragging a sweeps the construction continuously through no-triangle, right-triangle, two-triangle, and one-triangle outcomes, showing that the count of closures changes exactly at the thresholds a = h and a = b"
    },
    {
      "id": "showAltitude",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show altitude h",
      "default": true,
      "teaches": "The altitude h = b·sin α is the critical threshold: turning it on makes visible why a shorter than h can never reach the base (no triangle) and why a exactly equal to h lands perpendicular at β (the single right triangle)"
    },
    {
      "id": "caseStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step through cases",
      "states": ["No triangle", "Right triangle", "Two triangles", "One triangle"],
      "default": "No triangle",
      "teaches": "Advancing one named case at a time highlights the matching panel and its closing side or gap, connecting each inequality for a to the concrete geometric outcome it produces"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Fixed givens, swinging side",
      "narration": "Angle α and side b are fixed; side a is the free side that swings from γ. The altitude h = b·sin α is drawn as the reference threshold against which a will be compared.",
      "state": { "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "No triangle", "sideA": 4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "a < h — no triangle",
      "narration": "With a shorter than the altitude, the arc swept by a never reaches the base, leaving a visible gap — no triangle can close.",
      "state": { "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "No triangle", "sideA": 4 }
    },
    {
      "title": "a = h — right triangle",
      "narration": "When a exactly equals h, the swinging side just touches the base perpendicular to it at β, producing the single right triangle marked by the square.",
      "state": { "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "Right triangle", "sideA": 5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "h < a < b — two triangles (ambiguous case)",
      "narration": "For a between h and b, the arc crosses the base at two points, one on each side of the altitude foot, so two distinct triangles satisfy the same SSA data.",
      "state": { "showAltitude": true, "caseStep": "Two triangles", "sideA": 7 }
    },
    {
      "title": "a ≥ b — one triangle",
      "narration": "Once a is at least as long as b, only the far intersection remains on the base ray, collapsing the ambiguous case back to a single triangle.",
      "state": { "showAltitude": false, "caseStep": "One triangle", "sideA": 11 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The SSA 'ambiguous case' is not random — the count of valid triangles is fully determined by where the swinging side a falls relative to the altitude h = b·sin α and to side b.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Lay out four equal-width panels in a row, each with its case heading above and its (a)-(d) tag below, all sharing identical labeling: α with an angle arc at the left of a horizontal base, β at the right, γ at the apex, side b on the left slant, side a on the right. Use α = 30° and b = 10 so h = 5, giving clean thresholds. Panel (a) shows a as a short vertical stub from γ that stops above the base (clear gap); (b) shows a meeting the base at β with a right-angle square; (c) shows a solid vertical altitude labeled h plus two dashed 'a' segments to two base points; (d) shows a wide triangle with a sloping to β. Add a separate live construction strip (or overlay on the active panel) where side a rotates from γ as the slider moves, drawing the swept arc and marking intersection dots on the base. Color the altitude h and right-angle marker in a distinct blue, the swinging/dashed side a in red, and highlight the currently active panel with a soft background tint. Cross-fade the altitude guide on toggle rather than snapping."
}

